      ":question/id": "92f78b35-314c-56cd-8815-b47ddfe81b49",
      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-66963",
      ":question/problem": "What is the value of $x$ if ${e}^{2x+3}=\\sqrt[5]{7}?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we find the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2x+3} & =\\sqrt[5]{7} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}({e}^{2x+3}) & =\\operatorname{ln}(\\sqrt[5]{7})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $\\operatorname{ln}({e}^{a})=a$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives:\n\n$$\n2x+3=\\operatorname{ln}(\\sqrt[5]{7})\n$$\n\nNext, we write $\\sqrt[5]{7}$ as ${7}^{1/5}.$ So, we get:\n\n$$\n2x+3=\\operatorname{ln}({7}^{1/5})\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the right-hand side using power law of logarithms and solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+3 & =\\operatorname{ln}({7}^{1/5}) \\\\ 2x+3 & =\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}(7) \\\\ 2x & =\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}(7)-3 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}(7)-3) \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{ln}7-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{ln}7-\\frac{3}{2}.$"
    },
